Vaccines have led to the eradication of smallpox, a disease that once killed millions, demonstrating their effectiveness in preventing deadly illnesses.
Extensive scientific research and clinical trials consistently show that vaccines are safe and effective in preventing diseases and reducing their severity.
Mass vaccination programs have saved millions of lives globally, as evidenced by the dramatic decline in diseases like polio and measles.
The success of vaccines in reducing disease transmission and mortality is supported by decades of real-world data and public health outcomes.
When vaccination rates drop, diseases resurge, as seen with measles outbreaks in communities with low immunization coverage.